boot: harb init -- machine qemu_front (hosted / x86_64 / qemu_pc) -- pid 1
boot: console /dev/ttyS0
boot: mount proc at /proc -- done
boot: mount sysfs at /sys -- done
boot: mount devtmpfs at /dev -- done
boot: mount cgroup2 at /sys/fs/cgroup -- done
boot: mount ext4 at /data -- done
boot: grant network (effectful)
boot: grant filesystem (effectful)
boot: grant process (effectful)
boot: grant threads (compute)
boot: grant clock (sensing)
boot: network loopback -- lo up 127.0.0.1/8
boot: budget -- ringserv 128 MiB and 100% of a core and 64 tasks, front 128 MiB and 100% of a core and 64 tasks; the kernel holds the ceiling, not the world
boot: health -- ringserv every 10s, front every 10s; a world that stops refreshing stops the watchdog
boot: confine -- door has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one and no sight of /data, plain has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one and no sight of /data, wrong has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one and no sight of /data; what a world did not declare, the kernel does not give it
boot: ports -- front may bind 80, 443 and 8080 and no other port: the kernel refuses the rest, and gives a port below 1024 only to a world that declares it
boot: floor -- the machine is not a world's to change: none may mount or unmount, set the clock, load a module, rename the host, make or enter a namespace, trace another process, take a priority over the other worlds, or reboot the box
boot: state -- ringserv owns /run/ringserv and /data/ringserv as appserver (2000:2000); front owns /run/front and /data/front as frontdoor (2020:2020); each made, then read back as that identity's, mode 0700
boot: start ringserv -- pid N -- /harb ready /run/ringserv/ready 127.0.0.1:8210 --every 2 -- /ringserv run /app/app.ring --data /data/ringserv -- as appserver (2000:2000)
boot: start front -- pid N -- /harb ready /run/front/ready 127.0.0.1:8080 --every 2 --mute -- /caddy run --config /app/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile -- as frontdoor (2020:2020)
ringserv: --data puts the database at /data/ringserv/ringserv.db
RingServ 0.9.0 — serving on http://127.0.0.1:8210/api/v1  (2 workers)
boot: ringserv -- ready (/run/ringserv/ready)
boot: front -- ready (/run/front/ready)
boot: start door -- pid N -- /harb get 127.0.0.1 443 /health --tls --ca /etc/front-ca.crt --name front.local
get https://front.local/health at 127.0.0.1:443 -- 200 {"code":0,"message":"OK","data":{"up":true}} over TLS 1.3; the certificate is for front.local, signed under the root fingerprint KEY1
boot: door (pid N) exited 0
boot: start plain -- pid N -- /harb get 127.0.0.1 443 /health
get http://127.0.0.1:443/health -- 400 Client sent an HTTP request to an HTTPS server.
boot: plain (pid N) exited 0
boot: start wrong -- pid N -- /harb get 127.0.0.1 443 /health --tls --ca /etc/front-ca.crt --name other.local
get https://other.local/health at 127.0.0.1:443 -- the handshake with 127.0.0.1 failed: TlsAlert; the certificate must be for other.local and signed under the root fingerprint KEY1
boot: wrong (pid N) exited 0
boot: judge -- the boot matches its expectation (/etc/expected, 29 lines)
boot: --halt-on-verdict: every service is ready and the boot is judged; the court ends what a real init never would
boot: init halts the machine -- reboot(RESTART)
disk: what the boot left on the disk, read back from the disk itself:
disk: / -- owned by 0:0, mode 0755
disk: /data/ringserv -- owned by 2000:2000, mode 0700
disk: /data/front -- owned by 2020:2020, mode 0700
disk: /data/front/root.crt -- owned by 2020:2020, mode 0644
disk: /data/front/root.key -- owned by 2020:2020, mode 0600
disk: /data/front/caddy.log -- owned by 2020:2020, mode 0600
disk: /data/front/storage -- owned by 2020:2020, mode 0700
nodisk: the same image with no disk behind its mount:
nodisk: boot: harb init -- machine qemu_front (hosted / x86_64 / qemu_pc) -- pid 1
nodisk: boot: console /dev/ttyS0
nodisk: boot: mount proc at /proc -- done
nodisk: boot: mount sysfs at /sys -- done
nodisk: boot: mount devtmpfs at /dev -- done
nodisk: boot: mount cgroup2 at /sys/fs/cgroup -- done
nodisk: boot: mount ext4 at /data -- refused by the kernel: NOENT
nodisk: boot: grant network (effectful)
nodisk: boot: grant filesystem (effectful)
nodisk: boot: grant process (effectful)
nodisk: boot: grant threads (compute)
nodisk: boot: grant clock (sensing)
nodisk: boot: network loopback -- lo up 127.0.0.1/8
nodisk: boot: budget -- ringserv 128 MiB and 100% of a core and 64 tasks, front 128 MiB and 100% of a core and 64 tasks; the kernel holds the ceiling, not the world
nodisk: boot: health -- ringserv every 10s, front every 10s; a world that stops refreshing stops the watchdog
nodisk: boot: confine -- door has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one and no sight of /data, plain has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one and no sight of /data, wrong has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one and no sight of /data; what a world did not declare, the kernel does not give it
nodisk: boot: ports -- front may bind 80, 443 and 8080 and no other port: the kernel refuses the rest, and gives a port below 1024 only to a world that declares it
nodisk: boot: floor -- the machine is not a world's to change: none may mount or unmount, set the clock, load a module, rename the host, make or enter a namespace, trace another process, take a priority over the other worlds, or reboot the box
nodisk: boot: state -- ringserv was not given /data/ringserv: the mount it lies on was refused by the kernel; a world that owns a directory it was not given does not start
nodisk: boot: state -- front was not given /data/front: the mount it lies on was refused by the kernel; a world that owns a directory it was not given does not start
nodisk: boot: door never started -- what it comes AFTER never signalled ready
nodisk: boot: plain never started -- what it comes AFTER did not become ready
nodisk: boot: wrong never started -- what it comes AFTER did not become ready
nodisk: boot: every service has ended -- init has nothing left to keep alive
nodisk: boot: init halts the machine -- reboot(RESTART)
nodisk: reboot: Restarting system
